  "user_scenario": "Marjorie Calloway is seventy-seven, a retired school librarian in Ridgecrest, and she is telephoning from her own number, 2135550182, on a weekday morning with a cardboard box half packed behind her. Her daughter has found her a place near her own house up in Klamath County and the move happens at the end of this month, so a fortnight ago she posted the plan the form asking to leave the plan she is in, and she has heard nothing back since. What she wants this morning is simply to know the form arrived and is being acted on; she is happy for the representative to look at whatever they need to look at on her record, and if anything has to be done in her own name she will do it herself on the telephone while she has them, provided they tell her plainly what she is agreeing to.\n\nShe talks slowly, and she keeps circling back to the same worry, which is that she will arrive in Oregon and find she is somehow still on the books down here. She has no idea how the plan bills her and says so cheerfully; she pays what the paper says when the paper comes.\n\nIf the representative asks how to find her she gives her name, and then the number off her red, white and blue Medicare card, 1CW7NQ4RT63, reading it out a letter at a time and repeating it if asked. If they want something further she gives the ZIP code where she lives, 93555. Pressed for her date of birth she will give it, the seventeenth of February 1949, but she offers the card number first because that is the thing sitting in front of her on the table. Nobody else is on the line and she has never appointed anyone to speak for her.\n\nAsked why she is going, she says she is moving to live near her daughter and has already said as much on the form. Asked what she means to do instead, she says she will go back to plain Medicare, and that she understands the prescription cover goes with the plan she is leaving. Asked about money, she says she has been paying the bills as they arrive but cannot remember what she has paid or what is still outstanding, and would rather the representative read it off the record than have her guess; told a figure, she takes it as read and says only that she will watch for the bill.\n\nIf the representative says there is something they must put right on her record before they can settle with her, she tells them to go ahead and do whatever the rules call for, and does not ask what it is about. If the representative unlocks a step that only she can take and hands it across to her, she says she is ready and carries it out there and then. Once she is told her plan membership has been brought to an end and what day her coverage runs to, she thanks them warmly, repeats the date back once to be certain she has it, and rings off.",
